  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 328 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nA rather complex setup in the long term, with the main feature of \nconcern being the evolution of now Tropical Depression 9. Models \nremain highly confident in the development of the Depression likely \ninto a hurricane by Friday, however uncertainty is high when it \ncomes to the track, forward speed, and intensity over land. The \nupper level flow is rather unique with this system. The polar jet \nwill be well to the north Friday night into Saturday, with a large \nridge over the central CONUS. The subtropical jet, on the other hand, \nwill be stretched across the southern CONUS with a fast moving \nshortwave ejecting off of Texas and a wave break off the southern \nCalifornia coast. The eastern wave will likely provide enough shear \nto transition the tropical system to an extratropical one. However, \nmodels notoriously have trouble realizing the interactions between \ntwo systems of near equal size. Furthermore, the large amounts of \nadiabatic heat release will work to amplify the wave and add more \ncomplexity to the mix. \n\nSo there remain several unanswered questions as the system develops: \n \n1.) How much eastward momentum will the system have as it is \nlaunched northward by the developing shortwave? \n\n2.) How strong will the system be when this process starts? \n\n3.) How far east will the system make landfall?\n\nAll of these will determine the initial hazards of the system \nincluding tornado threat (how close are we to the front right \nquadrant?), wind threat, as well as the initial flash flooding \nthreat.\n\nAs we move into later Saturday, the concern will likely move towards \nheavy rainfall. Most models and ensembles bring the system almost \ndue north before re-curving under the larger ridge into the east-\ncentral CONUS. However, I would be a fool not to mention a few \nmodels which stand out. These include the Google GenCast, the 10/6 \n12z GFS, and the CMC, which stand out for a couple reasons. These \nmembers realized either a more eastward landfall closer to the \nMobile/Pensacola region, or deviant motion after landfall holding \nrain across the gulfcoast states. Now, this is just 3 members of a \nmuch larger set of models, however the dynamics behind these runs \nare not out of the realm of possibility and should still be kept on \nthe backburner. \n\nThe upper level feature to watch leading up to landfall will be the \nsubtropical shortwave off the California coast. This feature, a wave \nbreak pattern shown well in the 2PVU surface, will be very difficult \nfor models to represent well (especially given the 2 pacific \ntropical systems impacting it) and could initiate tertiary deviant \nmotion in the already complex shortwave/tropical interaction. This \nis really only true, though, in the case of a system with initially \nslower movement. The 18z GFS run shows this in its shift back west \npost-landfall, as the TD initialization realigned closer to the \nactual TD low center determined by the NHC. Should we see stalling \nin the system after landfall, the threat for both flash/riverine \nflooding and potentially tornadoes could change. \n\nDevelopment of the wedge may even further complicate things on \nSaturday due to high pressure off the northeast coast driving low \nlevel E flow around the Appalachians. Isentropic lift could focus \nprecip along the edge of the wedge and increased low level shear may \nenhance tropical rotation or cut it off from the surface.\n\nWidespread rainfall seems almost certain. The NBM ensemble indicates \na 70% chance of at least 1\" of rain over nearly the entire CWA, \nthough areas typically impacted by the wedge may have higher \nuncertainty.\n\nWhile the system may take a while to fully clear PoPs from the area, \nonce the shortwave kicks through conditions warm back into the\n80s.",
